    correct: "Screw a pressure gauge onto an outdoor hose bib with every fixture in the house turned off, and read the static pressure to find out whether the incoming supply pressure is too high.",
    wrong: [
      ["Install a low-flow aerator on the kitchen faucet to cut the splashing, since that is the fixture causing the problem.", "This jumps straight to a fix at one fixture while misreading the system: forceful splashing, banging pipes, and a weeping water-heater relief valve all point to high supply PRESSURE pushing on the whole house, not to one over-sized faucet. An aerator only masks the splash at that single tap and leaves the real stressor working on every seal, hose, and appliance in the home.", "Forceful flow at a fixture is a symptom of pressure, not a property of the aerator. Read the static pressure first so you know whether you are fighting the faucet or the supply itself."],
      ["Open the faucet all the way and time how long it takes to fill a five-gallon bucket to measure your flow rate in gallons per minute.", "A bucket test measures flow rate (GPM), but the clues here are about force and stress, which are governed by pressure (PSI). 'Tons of water' and 'too much force' are not the same thing. Measuring how much water comes out tells you nothing about why the pipes bang or why the relief valve drips, which are pressure-driven.", "You do not have a volume problem, you have a force problem. The right gauge is a pressure gauge read with everything OFF (static), not a stopwatch and a bucket measuring how much flows."],
      ["Replace the dripping valve on top of the water heater, since a leaking part is the clear thing to fix.", "The temperature-and-pressure relief valve is dripping because it is doing its job, relieving pressure that is too high. Swapping the valve treats the messenger, not the message. A brand-new valve fed by the same excessive pressure will simply drip again, and meanwhile the real cause remains undiagnosed.", "Before replacing a part that is reacting to the system, find out what the system is doing. Measure the static supply pressure so you learn whether the valve is faulty or just responding to a real overpressure."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Three different fixtures are complaining at once, so ask what they share before blaming any one of them, and notice the difference between how hard the water pushes and how much of it flows.",
    lesson: "Here is the \"aha\": pressure and flow are two different things, and the clues in this house are all about pressure, not about how much water you have. Pressure (measured in PSI) is the force shoving water through the pipes; flow rate (measured in gallons per minute) is how much actually comes out over time. A faucet that splashes violently, pipes that bang when you close a tap, and a water-heater relief valve that keeps weeping are the classic fingerprints of high static supply pressure, not of an unusually generous water supply. \"More pressure\" feels like \"more water,\" but it is really \"more force,\" and that force is what stresses every seal, washer, supply hose, and appliance in the home.\n\nThat is why the first move is to frame the system before touching any single part. A pressure gauge that screws onto an outdoor hose bib, read with every fixture in the house closed, gives you the static pressure: the resting force the municipal main (or a downhill location near a reservoir) is delivering to your house. Homes are generally happiest between about 40 and 80 PSI, with 60 PSI a common sweet spot. Most plumbing codes require a pressure-reducing valve once static pressure exceeds 80 PSI, precisely because sustained high pressure shortens the life of fixtures and appliances and triggers exactly the symptoms described. Reading that one number tells you whether you are dealing with a whole-house pressure issue or a quirk of a single fixture.\n\nNotice how each tempting \"fix\" skips this framing. The low-flow aerator, the bucket test, and the relief-valve swap all act on one component before anyone has confirmed which part of the system is even involved. Once you know the static pressure, the path forward is obvious: if it reads, say, 95 PSI, the real answer is a pressure-reducing valve at the main (often a pro job), and the dripping relief valve and banging pipes resolve on their own. If it reads a normal 55 PSI, then you genuinely have a localized fixture quirk and a different investigation begins. The gauge turns guessing into knowing.",
    solution: "Think of your water system like a garden hose connected to a tap. Two separate things are going on. One is how hard the water is being pushed, that is pressure, measured in PSI (pounds per square inch). The other is how much water comes out per minute, that is flow rate, measured in GPM (gallons per minute). They are related but not the same. You can have very high pressure and still only get a thin stream out of a clogged nozzle, and you can have gentle pressure feeding a wide-open pipe that pours out a lot of water. When your neighbor says \"tons of water,\" they are mixing up force with volume.\n\nThere is one more useful word: static pressure. \"Static\" just means \"at rest.\" Static pressure is what you read when nothing in the house is running, no faucets, no toilet refilling, no shower. It is the resting force the city main (or your downhill spot near a reservoir) is pushing against your closed pipes. That resting number is the single most telling clue about the whole house, which is why you read it first.\n\nNow look at the three complaints in this house. The faucet splashes hard (force). The pipes bang when you shut a tap (a pressure shock). The valve on top of the water heater drips (that valve, the temperature-and-pressure relief valve, opens when pressure climbs too high). Three different fixtures, one common cause: too much pressure pushing on everything. So the smart first step is not to change a part but to measure. You screw an inexpensive pressure gauge onto an outdoor hose bib, make sure every fixture inside is off, and read the dial. If it reads above about 80 PSI, you have found your culprit, and the real fix is usually a pressure-reducing valve installed where water enters the house, often a job for a pro. If it reads a comfortable 55 or 60 PSI, then the problem really is local to one fixture and you investigate differently. Either way, you measured before you touched, so you are fixing the right thing instead of chasing symptoms.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "A house at the foot of a hill has a kitchen faucet that sprays too hard, pipes that knock when taps close, and a water-heater safety valve that keeps dripping. Rather than replacing a fixture right away, what single measurement would best reveal whether the whole system is under too much force?" },
