    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "When your house was new, shutting off the upstairs faucet was silent. Lately, every time the washing machine's valve snaps shut, you hear a loud BANG in the wall — and you remember the plumber once mentioning the home has \"air chambers\" near the fixtures to prevent exactly this. The pipes are firmly strapped and nothing is loose. Given how those air chambers are supposed to work, what is the most likely reason the banging has returned, and the right first thing to try?",
    correct: "The air chambers have slowly filled with water (gone \"waterlogged\"), losing the air cushion that absorbs the shock — so shut off the main, open the highest and lowest faucets to drain the pipes, then turn the water back on to re-trap fresh air in the chambers.",
    wrong: [
      ["The pipes have worked loose over time, so the fix is to add more pipe straps and tighten the existing ones until the banging stops.", "This treats the symptom (a pipe rattling against framing) as the cause and ignores the principle: the BANG is a pressure shock wave from water's momentum slamming to a stop, not a loose pipe knocking wood. Straps may quiet a rattle but cannot absorb the pressure surge, and here the pipes are already firmly strapped.", "Ask what makes the noise possible at all: a fast-closing valve stops moving water instantly, and only a compressible air cushion can soak up that surge. Strapping doesn't add a cushion."],
      ["Household water pressure has crept too high, so the only real solution is to install or adjust a pressure-reducing valve to lower the whole system's pressure.", "High static pressure can worsen water hammer, but it doesn't explain why a home that was silent when new suddenly bangs — the air chambers were doing their job and then stopped. The principle says the shock needs somewhere to go; lowering baseline pressure doesn't restore the missing cushion.", "Separate the trigger (a valve stopping moving water) from the absorber (trapped air). The change over time points to the absorber failing, which a pressure-reducing valve does nothing to recharge."],
      ["The washing machine's solenoid valve is failing and slamming shut harder than it used to, so the banging means the appliance valve needs replacing.", "A solenoid valve closes fast by design from day one — that's normal and is exactly why the air chambers were installed. Blaming the valve ignores the principle that the system was built to absorb that fast closure; what changed is the cushion, not the valve.", "The fast shut-off is the expected trigger, not a defect. The right question is why the protection that used to cancel the bang has quit working, which points back to the air chambers."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Ask yourself what physically absorbs the shock of stopped water — and what happens to that absorber after a year or two of being in contact with the water.",
    lesson: "Water hammer is a momentum problem. Moving water in a pipe carries real momentum, and when a fast-acting valve (a washing machine or dishwasher solenoid, or even a quickly-shut single-handle faucet) stops that flow almost instantly, the water has nowhere to go. It piles up against the closed valve and sends a high-pressure shock wave ricocheting back through the pipe — the bang you hear and feel in the walls. The principle that holds: you cannot make that shock disappear by stiffening the plumbing; the energy has to be absorbed by something that can squish. Water itself is essentially incompressible, so the only thing that can cushion the blow is trapped air (or a sealed gas charge).\n\nThat is exactly what an air chamber is — a capped vertical stub of pipe near the fixture holding a pocket of air that compresses like a spring when the shock arrives. The catch is built into the design: that air is in direct contact with the water, and over months the water slowly absorbs the air until the chamber is full of water and has no spring left. Nothing is broken or loose; the cushion has simply filled up. This is why a home can be silent when new and start banging a year or two later. The honest first fix is free: kill the water at the main, open the highest faucet and a low one to let the pipes drain, then turn the water back on so fresh air gets retrapped in the chambers. If banging keeps returning quickly, the modern, maintenance-free answer is a mechanical water hammer arrestor — a sealed piston or bladder whose air charge never touches the water, so it cannot waterlog. Notice how each tempting wrong answer attacks something other than the cushion: tightening straps, lowering pressure, or blaming the valve. They each violate the core idea that the surge needs a compressible volume to absorb it, and that the thing which changed over time is precisely that volume disappearing.",
    solution: "Start with one stubborn fact: water in motion has momentum, and water barely compresses. So when a valve slams shut, the moving column of water hits a wall it can't squeeze into, and the energy bounces back as a pressure spike — a literal hammer blow inside your pipes. Fast-closing valves like the solenoids in washing machines and dishwashers are the classic triggers because they go from full flow to fully closed in a fraction of a second.\n\nBecause water won't compress, the only way to tame the spike is to give it a pocket of something that will: air. An air chamber is just a capped dead-end pipe holding trapped air that acts like a spring, absorbing the surge so the shock never reaches the walls as noise. The fatal flaw of the simple air chamber is that its air touches the water directly, and water gradually dissolves and carries that air away. Once the chamber is full of water, it's \"waterlogged\" and offers no cushion — which is why a quiet house can start banging after a year or two even though nothing came loose.\n\nThe free fix follows straight from the cause: drain the pipes so air can get back in. Shut off the main, open a high faucet and a low one, let everything drain, then restore the water — air gets retrapped in the chambers and the cushion returns. If it keeps waterlogging, upgrade to a mechanical water hammer arrestor, which seals its air or gas charge behind a piston or diaphragm so the water can never absorb it. The big takeaway: don't fight the bang by stiffening pipes, dropping pressure, or replacing a normal fast valve — restore or replace the air cushion, because that is the only thing physics lets absorb the shock.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "A homeowner whose plumbing used to be quiet now hears a thud in the wall whenever the dishwasher finishes filling. They have air chambers installed and tightly fastened pipes. Why has the noise come back, and what is the simplest thing they should do before buying anything?" },
