    mentorHint: "Think about the smallest valve that solves the problem, the one closest to the fixture itself, rather than the biggest valve in the house.",
    lesson: "Almost every modern fixture in a home, toilets, sinks, and many appliances, has its own little dedicated shutoff right where the water line meets it. On a toilet it's the egg-shaped or oval handle (or a small lever on newer quarter-turn models) on the wall behind the bowl, where the flexible supply line screws on. Plumbers call it an angle stop or fixture stop. Its entire reason for existing is so you can isolate one fixture and work on it without disturbing water service to the rest of the house. Turn it clockwise to close (or a quarter turn on a lever-style stop), flush to drain the tank, and the supply line goes dead while the kitchen, shower, and dishwasher all keep running normally.\n\nThe main shutoff is a different tool for a different scale of problem. It lives where water enters the house (often near the meter, in a basement, garage, or an outside box) and it cuts everything at once. You reach for it during a real emergency, a burst pipe, a flood, no working fixture stop, or when you're working on the supply line itself upstream of all the fixtures. Using it to change one fill valve is like cutting power to the whole house to change a single light bulb: it works, but it punishes the entire household and adds the chore of re-pressurizing and bleeding air from the lines when you're done.\n\nThe deeper habit worth building is \"isolate the smallest thing that solves the problem.\" Before any fixture repair, find that fixture's own stop first; the main is the fallback, not the default. One real-world caveat: older fixture stops can be crusty and may weep or refuse to seal fully when you finally turn them after years untouched. If yours won't hold, THEN you fall back to the main, and you've also just learned that that stop valve is itself due for replacement.",
    solution: "Big idea: plumbing is built in layers of control so you can shut off the smallest possible piece. The most powerful move is usually the smallest valve closest to the problem, not the biggest valve in the house.\\n\\nThe two layers you care about as a homeowner:\\n\\n1) Fixture stop valves (also called angle stops or supply stops). There's typically one behind every toilet and two under every sink (hot and cold). They isolate just that one fixture. Older ones are multi-turn (twist several times clockwise to close); newer ones are quarter-turn (a lever you rotate 90 degrees). This is your everyday repair tool: swapping a fill valve, a faucet, a supply line, or a toilet flapper assembly.\\n\\n2) The main shutoff. One valve where water enters the home that kills water to everything. This is your emergency and whole-system tool: burst pipes, major leaks, working on the main line, or when a fixture stop has failed and won't hold.\\n\\nThe practical routine before any fixture repair: (a) locate that fixture's own stop valve, (b) close it, (c) open the fixture, flush the toilet or open the faucet, to relieve the pressure already trapped in the line and confirm the water really stopped, (d) do your repair. If, and only if, the fixture stop won't shut the water off completely, fall back to the main, and put \"replace that bad stop valve\" on your list.\\n\\nA couple of myths to drop: hot-water valves don't affect toilets (toilets run on cold only), and holding the float down does not depressurize the supply line, so it won't keep water from spraying when you disconnect the line. Stopping water means closing a valve on the pipe, not fooling the float.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "It's Saturday and you're replacing the noisy fill valve in your upstairs toilet, a job that needs the toilet's water supply off for an hour or so while the rest of the family keeps using the house. Which single valve should you close so this toilet goes dry but every other tap and appliance still works?" },
